WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.576
Sorting a million items by comparing

2
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:08.109
every pair would take about five hundred

3
00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.100
billion comparisons.

4
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.374
There is a way to do the same

5
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.182
job in about twenty million.

6
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.835
That is about twenty-five thousand

7
00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.810
times less work.

8
00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.590
The method is called merge sort

9
00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:26.690
and it is a perfect example of recursion

10
00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:29.760
divide, conquer, combine.

11
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:31.980
This is merge sort.

12
00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:35.260
We'll see how to merge two sorted lists

13
00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.760
how recursion turns that into a full sort

14
00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:41.010
and why the total cost is n log n.

15
00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:43.340
Start with an easier problem.

16
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.480
Two lists are already sorted

17
00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.230
and we want one sorted list.

18
00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.530
Put a finger at the front of each list.

19
00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:53.550
Compare the two items under your fingers.

20
00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.470
The smaller one goes to the output

21
00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.140
and that finger moves on.

22
00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:03.800
Two, five, nine, against one, six, seven.

23
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:05.560
One is smaller.

24
00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.060
Then two.

25
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:08.520
Then five.

26
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.970
Then six, seven, and finally nine.

27
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.398
Each comparison puts one item in its place

28
00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:18.108
so merging n items takes at most

29
00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:19.810
n minus one comparisons.

30
00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.240
Here, six items, five comparisons.

31
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.570
Now the recursion.

32
00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.370
To sort a list, cut it in half

33
00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:29.610
sort each half

34
00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.420
and merge the two sorted halves.

35
00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:34.760
And how do we sort a half?

36
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:35.936
The same way.

37
00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:38.131
We keep cutting until each

38
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.840
piece has one item

39
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.610
because one item is already sorted.

40
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.510
Now all eight items are alone.

41
00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:49.110
Merge them back up: pairs, then fours

42
00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.320
then all eight.

43
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:52.750
Pause and predict.

44
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.250
Where does the actual sorting happen

45
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:58.580
when we split, or when we merge?

46
00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.500
Splitting just computes a midpoint.

47
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.290
Nothing is compared, and nothing moves.

48
00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:07.245
All the comparing happens in the merges.

49
00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:09.588
The recursion is only the scaffolding

50
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:11.750
that tells us which merges to do

51
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:13.400
and in which order.

52
00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.870
Now the cost.

53
00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:19.340
Let T of n be the work to sort n items.

54
00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.600
We sort two halves, and then merge

55
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.500
at most n comparisons.

56
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.300
So T of n equals two T of n over two

57
00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:29.290
plus n.

58
00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.443
Read it as a tree, like in the last video.

59
00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.407
Every level of the tree merges

60
00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.390
all n items once

61
00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.120
so every level costs about n.

62
00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:41.829
And there are log base two

63
00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.020
of n levels of merging

64
00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:46.840
because we halve until one item remains.

65
00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.950
n per level, log n levels: n log n.

66
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.190
Unrolling confirms it.

67
00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:57.370
T of n is two T of n over two, plus n.

68
00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:01.050
Again: four T of n over four, plus two n.

69
00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.590
After k steps

70
00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:05.690
two to the k times T of n over two to the k

71
00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:06.970
plus k n.

72
00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:11.210
At k equals log n, that is n times T of one

73
00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:14.420
plus n log n, which is order n log n.

74
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.030
Does the theory match the machine?

75
00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:20.315
Sorting a thousand random items takes about

76
00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:23.000
eight thousand seven hundred comparisons.

77
00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:25.600
Compare that with n log n

78
00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.553
which is about ten thousand

79
00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.097
and with the five hundred thousand

80
00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.990
of comparing every pair.

81
00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:34.749
Merge sort is slightly under n log n here

82
00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:36.450
because merging stops early

83
00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.340
when one list runs out.

84
00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.426
Merge sort has four properties

85
00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:42.640
worth remembering.

86
00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:44.020
First

87
00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:46.820
it always takes time of order n log n

88
00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.260
for any input.

89
00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.150
There is no bad case to worry about.

90
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.550
Second, it needs extra memory

91
00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.700
about n items, for the merged lists.

92
00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:00.440
Third, it is stable: if two items are equal

93
00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.835
the one that came first stays first

94
00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.522
because the merge takes from

95
00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:06.417
the left list on ties.

96
00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.268
That matters when you sort records

97
00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:10.486
by one field and want to keep an earlier

98
00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:11.930
order for the others.

99
00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:13.330
And fourth

100
00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.910
the recursion is only log n levels deep

101
00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:18.210
so the call stack stays tiny

102
00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:20.400
even for a million items.

103
00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.170
To sum up.

104
00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.390
Merge sort splits in half, sorts each half

105
00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:26.430
and merges.

106
00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.530
All the work is in the merge: n per level

107
00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:31.180
log n levels.

108
00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.220
Next time: quicksort

109
00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.980
which also divides and conquers

110
00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.300
but does its work before the recursion

111
00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:38.210
not after.

112
00:04:38.837 --> 00:04:41.207
Let's write merge sort in Python

113
00:04:41.207 --> 00:04:43.437
and count its comparisons.

114
00:04:44.017 --> 00:04:46.087
A list of zero or one item

115
00:04:46.167 --> 00:04:48.707
is already sorted: return it.

116
00:04:48.707 --> 00:04:50.987
Otherwise cut the list at the middle

117
00:04:50.987 --> 00:04:52.187
with floor division.

118
00:04:52.187 --> 00:04:54.407
Sort the left half and the right half

119
00:04:54.407 --> 00:04:56.447
each by calling merge sort again.

120
00:04:56.447 --> 00:04:59.017
Then merge the two sorted results.

121
00:04:59.367 --> 00:05:00.817
The slices copy

122
00:05:00.817 --> 00:05:04.037
so each call works on its own small lists.

123
00:05:04.037 --> 00:05:05.877
That keeps the code short

124
00:05:05.877 --> 00:05:07.847
at the price of extra memory.

125
00:05:08.427 --> 00:05:11.017
Merge is the two-finger idea.

126
00:05:11.017 --> 00:05:14.217
The variables i and j are the fingers.

127
00:05:14.217 --> 00:05:16.457
While both lists have items

128
00:05:16.457 --> 00:05:18.917
compare the two items under the fingers.

129
00:05:18.917 --> 00:05:21.867
The comparison is the line we count.

130
00:05:22.217 --> 00:05:24.767
The less-than-or-equal sign matters.

131
00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:27.727
On a tie we take the left item first

132
00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:30.577
and that is what makes merge sort stable.

133
00:05:30.927 --> 00:05:33.083
When one list runs out

134
00:05:33.177 --> 00:05:34.910
extend appends whatever is

135
00:05:34.977 --> 00:05:36.447
left of the other list.

136
00:05:37.027 --> 00:05:39.957
Here are real counts on random lists.

137
00:05:39.957 --> 00:05:41.985
For a thousand items

138
00:05:42.077 --> 00:05:43.043
eight thousand seven hundred

139
00:05:43.077 --> 00:05:44.517
fifteen comparisons.

140
00:05:44.517 --> 00:05:46.117
For two thousand

141
00:05:46.117 --> 00:05:48.787
about nineteen thousand four hundred.

142
00:05:49.137 --> 00:05:52.867
Each row stays below n log base two of n

143
00:05:52.867 --> 00:05:54.707
the number from the recursion tree.

144
00:05:54.707 --> 00:05:57.227
Double the size and the work doubles

145
00:05:57.227 --> 00:05:58.457
plus a little.

146
00:05:59.037 --> 00:06:01.647
In real programs, call sorted

147
00:06:01.647 --> 00:06:03.827
or the sort method of a list.

148
00:06:03.827 --> 00:06:05.627
Python uses Timsort

149
00:06:05.627 --> 00:06:08.597
which is built on merging, and it is stable.

150
00:06:08.947 --> 00:06:11.405
For merging several sorted inputs

151
00:06:11.477 --> 00:06:13.147
heapq dot merge does the

152
00:06:13.217 --> 00:06:15.547
two-finger idea lazily.

153
00:06:16.127 --> 00:06:20.997
So in Python: slices, a merge with fingers

154
00:06:20.997 --> 00:06:24.087
and a counter that confirms n log n.

155
00:06:24.437 --> 00:06:26.477
Next: quicksort.

